preConditionHook:
// THE BREAKOUT BEYOND THE WINDOW EXTREME FAILED. Long: within the last
// `reverseBars` bars the bar's low traded below the lowest low of the `lookback` bars
// ending just before that window, and this bar CLOSES back above that broken
// level by at least `toleranceTicks`. Short is the mirror above the highest
// high. Brooks's 'the first failed breakout below a prior swing low is a lower
// low buy setup'.
//
// THE LEVEL IS AN N-BAR WINDOW EXTREME, NOT A PIVOT: four of the six sources
// state it as a window and it needs no pivot retention, which is what drops this
// from hard to simple. The pivot-anchored statement of the same idea is
// SwingPointUndercut composed with a reclaim.
//
// NOT FailedBreakoutReversal, which is on disk: that rule needs a closing price below
// the level, re-measures the window per candidate bar, and carries no reclaim
// margin. This one fires on the intrabar low poking through a level fixed once,
// which is the far more common bar and the one Brooks is describing. Not
// StopRunReversalBar either, whose breakout bar is specifically the PRIOR bar
// and which demands a bull body on the reclaim.
//
// reverseBars defaults to 1 -- the single-bar rejection all three window sources
// describe -- and the loop is what lets the multi-bar reading (3) be reached by
// the grid. The level window is offset by reverseBars so no bar that is allowed
// to break it can also help define it. The extremes mirror Extremes(): seeded at
// the window's first bar, strict and toleranced (el_gt / el_lt), EL source.
//
// toleranceTicks defaults to 0, so the reclaim is the plain strict 'closed back
// above the level'; a positive value demands the close clear it by that much.
//
// Every scratch value is a localVariables entry, never a hook-scope
// declaration, so the hooks survive a double placement.
//
// Deepest bar touched: reverseBars + lookback - 1.
tickSize = ctx.MinMove() / ctx.PriceScale();
levelLow = low[(int)reverseBars];
levelHigh = high[(int)reverseBars];
for (ii = (int)reverseBars + 1; ii <= (int)reverseBars + (int)lookback - 1; ++ii) {
    if (el_lt(low[ii], levelLow)) levelLow = low[ii];
    if (el_gt(high[ii], levelHigh)) levelHigh = high[ii];
}
brokeDown = false;
brokeUp = false;
for (ii = 0; ii <= (int)reverseBars - 1; ++ii) {
    if (el_lt(low[ii], levelLow)) brokeDown = true;
    if (el_gt(high[ii], levelHigh)) brokeUp = true;
}
